[1mOriginal bugs preserved, not fixed:[22m[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstbds_siphash_bytes[0m builds each 8-byte word from [38;5;10mint[0m-typed sub-expressions, so a byte with the high bit set sign-extends and clobbers the upper 32 bits of [38;5;10mdata[0m. Consequence: keys of length 4–7 with [38;5;10md[3] >= 0x80[0m all hash identically. Reproduced via an explicit sign-extension helper.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstbds_hmput_key[0m's wrap-around match loop omits the [38;5;10mstbds_temp_key[0m update that the first loop performs.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstbds_hash_index::temp_key[0m is never initialized by [38;5;10mstbds_make_hash_index[0m. My first harness read it and both libraries segfaulted at the identical output byte; I removed the read to get coverage past it.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstbds_arrfreef(NULL)[0m still computes [38;5;10mNULL - 32[0m.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mstr_put[0m passes a 16-byte struct by value to [38;5;10mprintf("%s %d\n", ...)[0m. Under the SysV AMD64 ABI it occupies two INTEGER registers, so [38;5;10m%s[0m consumes [38;5;10mkey[0m and [38;5;10m%d[0m consumes the eightbyte holding [38;5;10mvalue[0m — the explicit third argument is never read. I pass [38;5;10mkey, value, value[0m to produce the identical register state; output is [38;5;10ma <num>[0m for every input.[0m[0m
